[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Lambda Expressions, Functional Interfaces, and Method References in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java 8 introduced powerful functional programming capabilities, fundamentally changing how developers write clean, concise, and efficient code. This tutorial explores lambda expressions, functional interfaces, and method references, showing how they work together seamlessly."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Functional Interfaces"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A functional interface is an interface that contains exactly one abstract method. They can have multiple default or static methods. The @FunctionalInterface annotation is used to enforce this rule at compile-time."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"@FunctionalInterface
public interface MathOperation {
    int calculate(int a, int b);
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Lambda Expressions"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"A lambda expression is an anonymous function that can be passed around as data. It removes the boilerplate of writing anonymous inner classes. The syntax consists of parameters, an arrow token (->), and a body."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public class LambdaDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Lambda expression implementing MathOperation
        MathOperation addition = (int a, int b) -> a + b;

        // Type inference allows omitting data types
        MathOperation multiplication = (a, b) -> a * b;

        System.out.println("10 + 5 = " + addition.calculate(10, 5));
        System.out.println("10 * 5 = " + multiplication.calculate(10, 5));
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Built-in Functional Interfaces"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java provides many standard functional interfaces in the java.util.function package. Key interfaces include:"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Predicate<T>: Takes one argument and returns a boolean."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consumer<T>: Takes one argument and returns no result (void)."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Function<T, R>: Takes one argument of type T and returns a result of type R."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Supplier<T>: Takes no arguments and returns a result of type T."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.function.Predicate;

public class PredicateDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Predicate<String> isLongerThanFive = s -> s.length() > 5;
        System.out.println(isLongerThanFive.test("Developer")); // true
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Method References"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Method references are shorthand notations for lambda expressions that call an already existing method. They use the double colon (::) operator and fall into four primary categories:"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to a static method: (ContainingClass::staticMethodName)"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to an instance method of a particular object: (containingObject::instanceMethodName)"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to an instance method of an arbitrary object of a particular type: (ContainingType::methodName)"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reference to a constructor: (ClassName::new)"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.List;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class MethodReferenceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Alice", "Bob", "Charlie");

        // Using a method reference instead of x -> System.out.println(x)
        names.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Lambda expressions, functional interfaces, and method references form the cornerstone of modern Java development. Mastering these concepts allows you to write declarative, highly readable code, especially when working with the Java Streams API."}]
